  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Find ${g}^{\u2032}(x)$ if $g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n${g}^{\u2032}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To compute ${g}^{\u2032}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={x}^{3}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[0,u].$ Therefore, by FTC II, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({u}^{3}-2u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})\\cdot (({x}^{3})^{3}-2({x}^{3})) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot ({x}^{9}-2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}({x}^{9}-2{x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
